{"description":"Encrypt payloads that rest in the coordinator's queue, so that a compromise of the staging server does not expose message bodies.\n\nScope is deliberately narrow, exactly as walkie/adr:004-security-model defines it: the coordinator's blind spots, not all traffic. Direct live traffic stays protected by WireGuard alone.\n\nConstruction is an X25519 sealed box with XChaCha20-Poly1305 AEAD from the audited standard extension libraries. No custom cryptography. The AEAD supplies message authentication, so no separate signature scheme is introduced.","acceptanceCriteria":"1. Each device generates an X25519 identity keypair on first run, stored with owner-only file permissions.\n2. Public keys distribute through the coordinator; the first key seen for a peer is pinned.\n3. A queued payload read directly out of the coordinator's database is ciphertext — verified by a test that inspects the stored bytes.\n4. A tampered ciphertext fails authentication and is rejected rather than partially processed.\n5. A changed key for an already-known peer produces a loud warning and requires explicit confirmation before use. Silent acceptance is a defect.\n6. A readable fingerprint is displayable for out-of-band verification.\n7. The documentation states that key loss forfeits that device's queued messages, and that key rotation and multi-device identity are not implemented."}